# {{ title }}

This note can use the databases in its own folder and subfolders, so `data/` is in scope. The `databases:` front matter gives them short names.

::toc{depth=2}

## Table

Every column, in the database's order:

::db[tasks]

Pick columns, filter rows and sort (a `-` sorts descending):

::db[tasks]{columns="Title,Status,Priority,Due" filter="Status != 'Done'" sort="-Priority,Due"}

Use a view saved in the database:

::db[tasks]{view=Open}

## Grouping

::db[tasks]{view="By owner"}

::db[tasks]{group=Status columns="Title,Owner,Due"}

## List

One line per row, with a few properties after the title:

::db[tasks]{type=list columns="Owner,Due" filter="Owner = 'Ada'"}

## Gallery

Cards with a cover image:

::db[projects]{type=gallery cover=Cover columns="Status,Lead"}

## Calendar

Rows placed on a month grid by their due date:

::db[tasks]{type=calendar date=Due}

## SQLite tables

The `crm` alias points at the `contacts` table of a SQLite file:

::db[crm]{view=Pipeline}

A table can also be named by path without an alias:

::db[data/crm.sqlite#deals]{columns="title,amount,closes_on" sort="closes_on"}

## Editing in the preview

In a trusted workspace you can edit these views directly:

- Click a cell to edit it. Select and status cells show a dropdown, and dates show a date picker.
- Click **New** to add a row, which gets a fresh `id`.
- Click a column header to sort for yourself, without changing this note.
- **Save view** writes the current sort and filter into the database as a named view.
- Formula cells such as `Overdue`, and the `Created` and `Updated` times, are read-only.

Edits to `.ydb` files can be undone in the editor. Edits to SQLite files ask for permission the first time in each session.
